WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:06.400
Elon Musk is now worth $167 billion&nbsp;
dollars. And depending on when you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.440
watching this video, he could be worth even more.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.480
Despite all the five star&nbsp;
hotels he could sleep in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:18.320
the Tesla CEO chooses to sleep at his&nbsp;
factories. Not just now and again, but often.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.160
That’s what he did during his recent visit to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.040
Gigafactory under construction in Berlin&nbsp;
- Tesla’s first vehicle factory in Europe

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.760
that will start turning out Model Ys this year.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:28.880
Where do you sleep tonight?

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.040
Tonight, it’s in the factory.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.680
In the factory? Not in a hotel?

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.240
Well, technically, a conference&nbsp;
room in the factory, but yeah.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.440
You sleep in the conference room in&nbsp;
the not finished factory tonight?

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.360
Yah, it gives me a good feel for what’s going on.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.080
Alone or...?

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.320
Yah, I assume so.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.680
Is this an invitation? (Laughter)

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.320
Kidding aside, he’s been doing this for years.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.560
Including camping on the roof of Tesla’s&nbsp;
battery factory in the Nevada desert.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.440
And he showed CBS exactly&nbsp;
where he slept at Tesla’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.920
manufacturing plant in Fremont, California.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.520
I sleep on the couch over there. Last time I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.240
here I actually slept literally on the&nbsp;
floor because the couch was too narrow.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.680
When things get really intense and I don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.160
time to go home and shower and&nbsp;
change, so I just sleep here.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.840
Saving time isn’t the only reason he&nbsp;
sleeps at work, it’s just his style.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.680
He explains that he doesn’t want to be&nbsp;
one of those CEOs in an ivory tower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.280
far removed from the hardships his&nbsp;
employees face on the frontline.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.000
He says he wants his pain to be worse than theirs.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.600
There’s no question, his employees have had to&nbsp;
endure a lot to get Tesla to where it is today.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.000
The company delivered nearly half&nbsp;
a million cars in 2020 - a stunning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.480
achievement considering how close it&nbsp;
was to bankruptcy a few short years ago

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.040
as it struggled to mass-produce the Model 3.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.600
In 2018, Tesla planned on&nbsp;
producing 5,000 Model 3s every week

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.320
but only managed about 800 a week in&nbsp;
the first three months of that year.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.560
Frankly, we’re gonna be in&nbsp;
production hell. Welcome, welcome.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.000
Part of the problem was that Tesla&nbsp;
relied on too much automation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.520
or, too many robots, too early. And any issue&nbsp;
would cause the entire assembly line to stop.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.880
The human staff was also overworked, exhausted,&nbsp;
and according to a report in the Guardian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.280
even passing out on the factory floor.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.800
In response to this article,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.240
Musk said he cared deeply about the&nbsp;
health and safety of his employees

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.800
and emphasized that Tesla’s safety record&nbsp;
had improved significantly since then.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.680
But shortly after, Tesla was hit by another&nbsp;
claim of high injury rates at the factory.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.080
So Musk responded with an all-staff email&nbsp;
that’s been applauded by leadership experts.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.800
He wrote: No words can express how much&nbsp;
I care about your safety and wellbeing.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.720
It breaks my heart when someone is injured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.000
building cars and trying their&nbsp;
best to make Tesla successful.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.160
He went on to say he’d meet with every&nbsp;
injured employee and would "...go down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.360
to the production line and perform&nbsp;
the same task that they perform.”

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.560
It’s not certain whether he actually&nbsp;
met with every single injured person

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.800
but this hands-on approach was clear evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.560
that he’s willing to put himself at&nbsp;
the very heart of problem-solving.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.640
And sleeping in his factories could go a&nbsp;
long way toward inspiring his employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.200
to make Tesla an overwhelming success

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.400
by helping the world transition&nbsp;
to sustainable energy.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:19.840
Tesla aims to produce 20 million cars a year by&nbsp;
2030 - that’s 40 times what it produced in 2020.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.440
Only an insane amount of hard&nbsp;
work will get Tesla there.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.240
Musk says at times he’s clocked in more than 120&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.760
hours a week - a work ethic&nbsp;
that he adopted early on.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.480
When he and his brother Kimbal&nbsp;
founded their first company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.200
Zip2, which was like an internet version of&nbsp;
the yellow pages, they only had one computer.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.480
So the website ran during the&nbsp;
day while they coded at night.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.840
They also slept at the office because they didn’t&nbsp;
have an apartment and showered at the YMCA.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.600
He doesn’t expect his employees&nbsp;
to camp out at work but does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.080
expect them to spend a lot of waking hours there.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:58.080
During that painful production ramp-up of the&nbsp;
Model 3, staff was working 100 hours a week.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.840
That’s the equivalent of 14&nbsp;
hours a day, seven days a week.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:05.040
Critics say he pushed his workers&nbsp;
to the brink. Musk said there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.440
no other way to meet Tesla’s production targets.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.600
The company bounced back and exceeded its&nbsp;
delivery goals in the second half of 2019.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.960
Tesla is now the most valuable car company in&nbsp;
the world as its stock price continues to soar.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:25.760
Musk’s rocket company SpaceX is&nbsp;
also soaring - literally - as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.800
it tests out prototypes of&nbsp;
the massive Starship system

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.040
that plans to send people to the moon and Mars.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.640
Even though his companies’ futures look&nbsp;
bright, the CEO says it’s no time to slow down.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:41.440
He once explained that if someone is working&nbsp;
50 hours a week and you’re working 100,

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.600
you’ll get twice as much done in the course&nbsp;
of a year and be that much further ahead.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.960
Perhaps this mentality is the&nbsp;
reason that on Glassdoor reviews,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:55.680
only 65% of Tesla employees recommend working&nbsp;
there compared with 85% who recommend Apple.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.320
That doesn’t mean people don’t&nbsp;
want to be hired at Tesla.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.200
They just recognize it’s&nbsp;
a demanding place to work.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.800
As Musk himself has admitted, “There&nbsp;
are way easier places to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.000
but nobody ever changed the&nbsp;
world on 40 hours a week”.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.480
So his couch-sleeping will&nbsp;
probably continue. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.960
he might find it somewhat more comfortable now.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:19.520
A YouTuber named Ben Sullins started&nbsp;
a GoFundMe to get Musk a bigger couch.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:22.800
A furniture company caught wind&nbsp;
of it and offered one up for free.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:24.960
The money raised ended up going to charity.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.320
So, when do you think Tesla will be&nbsp;
able to produce 20 million cars a year?

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.920
Do you think it'll happen by&nbsp;
2030? Earlier? Later? Let me know.

